myuri (Go port), in Camoufox's goapi
====================================

This package is a Go port of two TypeScript libraries, which it follows
function by function so that one seed makes the same input as theirs:

  - @jwriter20/myuri 4.0.0, https://github.com/JWriter20/Myuri
    © Jake Writer, licensed under the GNU Lesser General Public License v3.0 or
    later (LICENSE next to this file). The smart actions, the planners and the
    timing models.

  - cursory-js 2.1.0, https://github.com/JWriter20/cursory-js
    A port of cursory (https://github.com/Vinyzu/cursory), © Vinyzu, dual-licensed
    under LGPLv3-or-later and GPLv3-or-later. The port is © Jake Writer, LGPLv3-or-later.
    The mouse trajectories.

Camoufox as a whole is MPL-2.0. The files in this directory are not: they remain
LGPLv3-or-later and are identified as such here, which is what section 4 of that
licence asks of a work that combines them.

Bundled data
------------

trajectories.json.gz is the recorded-trajectory database of cursory, as shipped by
cursory-js 2.1.0, byte for byte. The recordings behind it are credited by the
original author to:

  - Margit Antal, Norbert Fejer and Krisztian Buza, for SapiMouse
    (https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/9465583)
  - MIMIC-LOGICS, for Mouse-Synthesizer
    (https://github.com/MIMIC-LOGICS/Mouse-Synthesizer)

page_functions.json holds the source of myuri 4.0.0's SCROLL_PLAN_JS and HIT_TEST_JS, the two
functions it runs in the page, as testdata/gen/gen.mjs writes it from the pinned package.

Typing
------

The typo and correction model (typing_markov.go) follows myuri's port of
HumanTyping (https://github.com/Lax3n/HumanTyping). bigrams.go embeds a table
derived by myuri from english_bigrams.txt in John Burkardt's NGRAMS dataset
(https://people.sc.fsu.edu/~jburkardt/datasets/ngrams/ngrams.html), distributed
under the GNU LGPL. testdata/gen/tables.mjs regenerates it from the pinned
package.

Derived from numpy
------------------

To reproduce cursory's output for a given seed, cursory-js reimplements the parts of
numpy that its results depend on, and this port follows it: the PCG64 bit generator
and SeedSequence, the ziggurat normal sampler and its constant tables (ziggurat.go),
Lemire's bounded-integer method, and pairwise summation. Those algorithms and the
ziggurat tables come from numpy (https://github.com/numpy/numpy), © 2005-2025 NumPy
Developers, BSD-3-Clause. numpy's PCG64 is itself © 2014 Melissa O'Neill and
© 2015 Robert Kern, MIT licensed.

hypot follows CPython's vector_norm (https://github.com/python/cpython), © 2001-2025
Python Software Foundation, PSF License v2.
